{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VQpmUqAWmkf5pW5JC8xDVy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CHG9jR1WJbcvWv4apuptnV","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: OntoCoDec\n\n**Paper:** OntoCoDec: Near-Complete Type-Valid Information Extraction via Compiled Ontology Constraints\n**Venue:** EMNLP 2026 (ARR)\n**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)\n**Date:** 2026-05-14\n\n---\n\n## Venue Scoring Basis\n\nEMNLP 2026 uses ACL Rolling Review (ARR). Three scoring dimensions (1–5 scale): **Soundness** (technical rigor), **Excitement** (novelty/impact), **Overall Assessment** (composite recommendation). Criteria sourced from [ARR Review Form](http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform) and [ARR Reviewer Guidelines](http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines).\n\n---\n\n## Step 1: Independent Reviews\n\n### Reviewer 1 (R1) — Methods & Experiments\n\n**Scores:** Soundness=4, Excitement=3, Overall=3, Confidence=4\n\n**Strengths:**\n- **Thorough ablation design.** The 4-way ablation (unconstrained / grammar-only / semantic-only / full S1) on both datasets cleanly isolates each layer's contribution. The enabling dependency claim is well-supported: semantic-only achieves only 24% DR reduction on SciERC vs. 94% for the full system, with a convincing mechanistic explanation (OOV relation names block domain/range lookup).\n- **Honest treatment of negative results.** CF trade-off (9.90% SciERC, 12.02% Llama-DocRED), grammar-only DR *increase* on DocRED (18.46→26.17%), and S2 null result are all reported transparently with structural explanations.\n- **Statistical rigor.** 10,000-iteration bootstrap with CIs, McNemar tests for prompt comparison, per-dataset significance reporting (SciERC p=0.58 neutral, DocRED p<0.001 significant) rather than cherry-picking.\n- **Cross-model 2×2 validation** with genuine dual-factor insight (DR reduction mirrors RV baseline ordering).\n- **Post-hoc baseline** framed as multi-dimensional comparison (recall, coverage, TP loss) rather than F1 contest. The 127 lost TPs on DocRED is compelling.\n- **Constraint tightness analysis** provides a clean causal chain: tightness → fallback rate → residual DR.\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n- **2×2 scope narrow for generality of claims.** Two 8B-class models, two datasets, zero-shot only. No larger model (e.g., 70B), no fine-tuned model. The practical motivation for constrained decoding is arguably stronger in the fine-tuned regime where F1 is meaningful.\n- **Very low absolute F1 (~8% R-F1) raises ecological validity questions.** Constraint enforcement on a distribution ~92% wrong may exhibit different dynamics than on a higher-accuracy regime. The tightness-fallback-DR relationship may not hold when the base model is more accurate.\n- **CF trade-off substantial and severity distinction unvalidated.** DR violations framed as \"hard\" and CF as \"soft,\" but no downstream task evaluation confirms this. The dominant pattern (Generic↔OtherScientificTerm) is attributed to type system ambiguity — this risks dismissing a method limitation as a dataset artifact.\n- **Decidability taxonomy limited novelty.** The local/prefix-decidable/non-local distinction is well-known in constraint satisfaction and compiler literature. Presented as a contribution but reads as design rationale.\n- **DocRED 10.21% residual DR (Qwen) strains \"near-complete\" title.** One of four matrix cells retains >10% violations.\n- **No comparison with fine-tuned or training-based methods.** Even a few-shot or LoRA baseline would contextualize the practical value.\n- **Semantic-only ET increase on DocRED (2.48→4.22%) concerning.** Partial constraint application making a different violation category worse is a negative interaction effect that warrants deeper discussion.\n\n**Key Questions:**\n1. Is the fallback-rate / residual-DR near-exact match (DocRED ~10.2% each) a mathematical tautology or an empirical finding?\n2. Would CF persist with a fine-tuned model producing more accurate types?\n3. What happens when first-token masking uniqueness assumption fails (different tokenizer, larger type vocabulary)?\n4. Parse failure increase for Llama-DocRED (68→91): can increasing max_new_tokens eliminate this?\n5. Grammar-only R-F1 on DocRED (8.31) > Full S1 (8.23) — does the semantic layer introduce systematic negative F1 pressure?\n\n---\n\n### Reviewer 2 (R2) — Novelty & Related Work\n\n**Scores:** Soundness=4, Excitement=2, Overall=3, Confidence=4\n\n**Strengths:**\n- **Violation landscape study is a genuine empirical contribution.** No prior work has systematically characterized per-relation ontology constraint violation distributions in LLM-based IE. The tightness-violation correlation (SciERC 7 relations at 15.4% DR ≈ DocRED 96 relations at 18.5% DR) is non-obvious. The Evaluate-for regression (+5.8pp under schema prompting) is particularly compelling.\n- **Related work is thorough.** Six organized paradigms with ~42 references: grammar-constrained decoding, semantic constrained decoding for code, vocabulary/type-constrained generation, structural constraints via training, post-hoc ontology validation, and quality impact of constrained decoding.\n- **PICARD differentiation well-articulated.** Clean technical distinction: PICARD operates over fixed, fully enumerated database schemas, while OntoCoDec's entity registry is populated during generation from free-text spans.\n- **Honest positioning.** Title change from \"Guaranteed\" to \"Near-Complete,\" proactive CF disclosure, dual-factor model more insightful than simple tightness-only narrative.\n- **Cross-model validation meaningful.** The RV–DR orthogonality (Llama: low RV but high DR; Qwen: opposite) justifies the two-layer design from a model-agnostic perspective.\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n- **(Major) Core technical contribution has limited novelty.** Extending grammar-constrained decoding with a stateful semantic layer is a domain transfer of techniques from code generation (PICARD EMNLP'21, Synchromesh ICLR'22, MGD NeurIPS'23, PLDI'25 type systems → prefix automata) to a structurally *simpler* constraint system. IE has flat domain/range sets over small entity type inventories (6 types) vs. deep subtyping and parametric polymorphism in PL. The entity registry is a dictionary lookup, not a novel inference mechanism. Composition via bitwise AND is the standard approach.\n- **(Major) Decidability taxonomy limited conceptual depth.** The local / prefix-decidable / non-local classification follows directly from well-known properties of autoregressive left-to-right generation. Similar taxonomies appear implicitly in Synchromesh and MGD. The paper would benefit from either rigorous formalization (connecting to formal language theory) or honest positioning as organizational framework rather than standalone contribution.\n- **(Moderate) CRANE cited but not empirically compared.** CRANE occupies the space between post-hoc filtering and single-pass constrained decoding. The paper notes S2's two-pass strategy is \"analogous to CRANE's alternating strategy\" but provides no comparison.\n- **(Moderate) Missing connections to neuro-symbolic and structured prediction literature.** DeepProbLog, Semantic Loss, posterior regularization, constraint relaxation literature — OntoCoDec's approach (compiling declarative constraints into procedural enforcement) is a specific instance of this broader research direction.\n- **(Moderate) \"Enabling dependency\" inflated.** That grammar must validate relation names before semantic can look up rules is an architectural consequence of the design, not a deep insight about constraint composition.\n- **(Minor) \"First systematic study\" should be scoped** to \"first systematic study of ontology constraint violations in zero-shot LLM-based IE.\"\n- **(Minor) Missing citations:** TANL (Paolini et al., ICLR 2021), REBEL (Cabot & Navigli, EMNLP 2021), SynCode (Ugare et al., 2024).\n\n**Key Questions:**\n1. What distinguishes OntoCoDec from PLDI'25 applied to a simpler constraint structure? What IE-specific technical challenge required a novel solution?\n2. How does OntoCoDec differ from EnriCo (Zaratiana et al., 2024), which also enforces type constraints at generation time using ASP?\n3. What happens when the model generates an entity in a relation not listed in the entity list? How does the registry handle missing entries?\n4. Do violation landscape findings generalize beyond zero-shot? If fine-tuned models achieve low baseline DR, the practical motivation narrows substantially.\n5. How sensitive is the tightness metric to using observed type pairs vs. the Cartesian product of dom(r) × range(r)?\n\n---\n\n## Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n\n### R1's Response to R2\n\n**Agreements:**\n- R2's framing of OntoCoDec as \"domain transfer to structurally simpler constraints\" is sharper than R1's original concern. R1 concedes this is the paper's most vulnerable flank.\n- CRANE empirical comparison gap aligns with R1's missing-baselines concern.\n- \"First systematic study\" should be scoped.\n- Q4 (generalization beyond zero-shot) strengthens R1's ecological validity concern.\n\n**Disagreements:**\n- R1 defends the **enabling dependency** as having empirical value: the 24% vs. 94% gap is non-trivially large, even if architecturally predictable. The quantitative magnitude is the contribution.\n- R1 considers R2's **Excitement=2 too harsh**. The violation landscape study is a genuine first, 94% DR reduction at zero F1 cost is practically useful, and the system works with honest limitations reported.\n\n**Score revision:** R1 maintains Soundness=4, Excitement=3, Overall=3, Confidence=4.\n\n### R2's Response to R1\n\n**Agreements:**\n- R1's narrow-scope concern reinforces R2's novelty concern from the experimental side.\n- 8% R-F1 ecological validity aligns with R2's unstated practical deployment concern.\n- DocRED 10.21% residual DR matches R2's \"near-complete\" skepticism.\n- CF severity distinction unvalidated — shared concern.\n\n**Disagreements:**\n- R2 finds R1 **too generous on the ablation**: the enabling dependency is architecturally self-evident rather than a discovery.\n- R2 finds R1 **too lenient on statistical rigor**: DR/CF metrics are single-run point estimates with no CIs. The \"94% DR reduction\" headline has no confidence interval.\n\n**New insights from R1:**\n- R1's Q1 (fallback-rate/residual-DR tautology) is sharp — if the match is architectural, the \"tightness predicts DR\" claim is partially circular.\n- Llama RV-vs-DR orthogonality partially addresses R2's novelty concern on the empirical side.\n\n**Score revision:** R2 would move Excitement to 2.5 if half-points permitted; holds at 2 on integer scale. Maintains Soundness=4, Overall=3.\n\n---\n\n## Step 3: Convergence\n\n### Agreed Points (Both Reviewers)\n\n| Dimension | Consensus |\n|-----------|-----------|\n| **Soundness** | 4 (Strong). Experimental design is rigorous: ablation cleanly isolates layers, statistical analysis is proper, negative results honestly reported. |\n| **Overall** | 3 (Borderline). Technically solid but bounded contributions. |\n| **Violation landscape** | Genuine empirical contribution; first systematic characterization of per-relation DR distributions in LLM-based IE. |\n| **Ablation quality** | Well-designed 4-way ablation on two datasets with cross-model validation. |\n| **Honest reporting** | CF trade-off, grammar DR increase on DocRED, S2 null result, post-hoc F1 advantage — all transparently disclosed. |\n| **CF unvalidated** | DR \"hard\" / CF \"soft\" severity distinction asserted but not empirically confirmed via downstream evaluation. |\n| **Scope narrow** | 2 models × 2 datasets × zero-shot only insufficient for \"model-agnostic\" / broad IE claims. |\n| **\"Near-complete\" strained** | 10.21% residual DR on Qwen-DocRED contradicts title framing. |\n| **CRANE comparison missing** | Cited but not empirically compared; notable gap. |\n| **Decidability taxonomy** | Limited novelty; useful as organizational framework, not as standalone theoretical contribution. |\n\n### Disagreed Points\n\n| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Resolution |\n|-----------|-----|-----|-----------|\n| **Excitement** | 3 (Borderline) | 2 (Mediocre) | Split. R1 weights practical utility; R2 weights novelty gap vs. code-gen literature. **Consensus: 2.5 → round to 3** with explicit caveat that novelty is the primary risk factor. |\n| **Enabling dependency** | Empirically validated (24% vs 94% gap is non-trivial) | Architecturally self-evident | **Consensus:** The quantitative gap adds modest value but should not be listed as a standalone contribution. |\n| **Statistical rigor** | Praised (bootstrap, McNemar) | Insufficient (DR/CF lack CIs) | **Consensus:** F1 analysis is statistically rigorous; violation rate metrics lack uncertainty quantification. Mixed verdict. |\n\n---\n\n## Step 4: Consolidated Assessment\n\n### Final Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Soundness** | **4** (Strong) | Experimental design is thorough: 4-way ablation isolating each layer, 2×2 cross-model matrix, 10,000-iteration bootstrap for F1, McNemar tests for prompt effects, post-hoc multi-dimensional comparison. Claims are carefully calibrated to evidence. Negative results honestly reported. Gap: violation rate metrics lack uncertainty quantification; no downstream CF impact evaluation. |\n| **Excitement** | **3** (Borderline) | The violation landscape study is a genuine empirical first. The system achieves strong DR reduction (94% on SciERC) at zero F1 cost with an honest CF trade-off analysis. The dual-factor model and RV–DR orthogonality are real analytical contributions. However, the core technical mechanism (stateful logit masking for domain/range constraints) is domain transfer of techniques well-established in code generation (PICARD, Synchromesh, PLDI'25) to a structurally simpler constraint system. The decidability taxonomy restates known properties of autoregressive generation. Novelty is the primary risk factor. |\n| **Overall** | **3** (Borderline) | A well-executed systems paper with clean experiments and honest reporting, filling a genuine gap (no prior work compiles IE ontology domain/range constraints into decoding-time masks). The violation landscape study, thorough ablation, cross-model validation, and dual-factor analysis are all positives. However: (1) limited novelty of the core framework — competent domain transfer, not new algorithms; (2) narrow scope (2×2, zero-shot only, ~8% F1); (3) substantial CF trade-off without downstream validation; (4) 10% residual DR on DocRED strains \"near-complete\" framing. Above the bar for **Findings**; borderline for main conference. |\n| **Confidence** | **4** (Quite sure) | Both reviewers are familiar with constrained decoding, IE methods, and the code-generation constraint literature. |\n\n### Recommendation\n\n**Borderline — lean toward Findings track rather than main conference.**\n\nThe paper makes a genuine empirical contribution (violation landscape study, tightness-driven analysis) and demonstrates a working system with clean experiments. The main weakness is that the technical framework itself — compiling ontology constraints into grammar + semantic decoding masks — is a natural and relatively straightforward adaptation of techniques already established in code generation to a simpler constraint domain. EMNLP main track typically expects either a more novel technical contribution or a substantially broader empirical study.\n\n### Key Revisions That Would Strengthen the Paper\n\n1. **Add a fine-tuned or few-shot baseline** to establish whether the violation landscape and constraint enforcement benefits hold in a higher-accuracy regime. This is the single most impactful addition.\n2. **Downstream CF impact evaluation.** Run the constrained vs. unconstrained output through a downstream task (e.g., question answering over the extracted KG) to empirically validate the DR-hard/CF-soft severity distinction.\n3. **CRANE empirical comparison.** Compare against constrained re-generation, not just post-hoc filtering.\n4. **Add CIs for violation rate metrics.** The headline \"94% DR reduction\" currently has no confidence interval.\n5. **Scope claims more carefully.** \"Near-complete\" is justified on SciERC (0.93%) and Llama-DocRED (2.55%) but not on Qwen-DocRED (10.21%). The title and abstract should reflect this variance.\n6. **Sharpen novelty positioning vs. code-gen literature.** A dedicated paragraph explaining what IE-specific challenge required a solution beyond what PICARD/Synchromesh/PLDI'25 already provide.\n\n### Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nAll planned experiments from the design document are reflected in the paper:\n- **Phase 0 (violation landscape):** P001–P004 ✓ (Tables 1–3, §3)\n- **Phase 2 (ablation):** P005a, P005–P008 (SciERC), P009–P012 (DocRED) ✓ (Tables 4–7, §5.2–5.4)\n- **Cross-model:** P013–P016 ✓ (Table 8–9, §5.5)\n- **Post-hoc baseline:** P018 ✓ (Table 10, §5.6)\n- **Bootstrap CI:** P019 ✓ (Table 11, §5.7)\n- **S2 pilot (P017):** Correctly omitted (engineering checkpoint)\n- **P020:** Correctly omitted (killed)\n\nCoverage is complete. The paper reflects all non-killed experiments from the design.\n\n### Summary\n\nOntoCoDec is a technically competent paper with above-average experimental rigor and commendable honesty about its limitations. The violation landscape study and the dual-factor analysis are the strongest intellectual contributions. The framework itself works as described but represents domain adaptation of known constrained-decoding techniques rather than algorithmic innovation. The 2×2 zero-shot evaluation, while internally thorough, leaves open the question of practical utility in the regime where IE systems are actually deployed (fine-tuned, higher accuracy). The CF trade-off, while honestly reported, lacks downstream validation of the asserted severity distinction. These factors place the paper at the borderline for EMNLP main conference, with a clear path to strengthening through broader evaluation and sharper novelty positioning.\n","project":"schema_semantic_constrained_ie","tier":"best","dream_id":"20260514T205155","overall_score":"borderline","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1189,"cache_read_input_tokens":75493,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1189},"output_tokens":50,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d100b6b0-1eb0-4d27-a3fa-08cfd6c31175","uuid":"4d714db5-8a18-4d49-b889-7d4725f29321"}
